  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAn initial upper-level disturbance has spread lots of mid clouds\nacross the region today, and has tried to produce some light\nshowers or sprinkles, especially over southeast Missouri.\nGuidance has some 20+% PoPs, so threw in a slight chance of\nmeasurable showers with some sprinkles surrounding it this\nafternoon. The light shower chances will spread from the \nMississippi River northeast through the Tri State through the\nafternoon.\n\nA more substantial disturbance will bring scattered to numerous\nshowers to the region later this evening through much of Sunday.\nInstability will mostly remain well to the south of the region,\nbut a stray rumble of thunder cannot be ruled out along the\nsouthern border of the region overnight, especially near the\nBootheel. Ensembles indicate that the precipitable water values\nremain near 200% of normal, so there certainly is enough\nmoisture to be of concern for heavy rainfall and flooding.\nHowever, the forcing will not be strong enough or persist long\nenough to support a serious concern this weekend. \n\nAs for timing, it appears that the initial surge of showers\nnorthward into the area will occur in the 0-3Z time frame,\nmainly over southeast Missouri and the Purchase Area of \nKentucky. The main rain event throughout the region is expected \nfrom 03Z-18Z Sunday. Forecast rainfall amounts will range from \naround 0.66\" in the far south to near zero north of I-64. \nShowers may linger through Sunday afternoon near the eastern \nborder of the region. Some isolated light showers will then \nlinger through Sunday night, mainly over west Kentucky.\n\nHigh pressure aloft should keep the area dry Monday into early\nTuesday, as our next storm system organizes over the Plains.\nForecast winds on Tuesday have trended downward and are not\nexpected to push Advisory criteria (40mph). \n\nGuidance is in reasonable agreement in a north/south oriented \nband of showers ahead of a cold front moving slowly eastward \nacross Quad State Tuesday afternoon through at least midday\nWednesday. Instability will once again be limited with the upper\nsystem well north of the region, but the NBM does have a \nmention of thunder for much of this period. Precipitable water \nvalues remain around 200% of normal, so with the slow eastward \nmotion of the band, some heavy rainfall and isolated flooding \nissues may develop. The current forecast rainfall ranges from \naround 2\" in the southeast to around 1\" in the northwest. The\n12Z ensembles only have 20-40% chance of reaching just 1\" of\nrainfall, so the current forecast is well to the high side of\nthe latest guidance.\n\nThe front should pass at some point Wednesday and that will push\nthe low-level moisture east of the Quad State. While the upper-\nlevels will remain active with plenty of energy streaming\nby, the NBM has rain chances gradually tapering off by Friday.\nBased on the latest guidance, the forecast may be dry by\nThursday.",
